    "created_at": "2025-05-15T23:10:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-05-15T23:10:48Z",
    "body": "I think 1 is the most realistic and will give you the most control especially if you're looking to do immediate playback. i.e. playback audio as soon as it's available. Otherwise, if you're stitching together the various audio chunks into a single file, it will yield a seamless playback. \n\nEven with 2, you'd still have this issue it would just take longer to get to.\n\nA potential issue I see with 3 is, regardless of the pause, you need to have this audio chunk queued up and play immediately following the end of the previous audio chunk which may be a bit tricky to get right IMO.\n\nA slight variation to 1 may be to chunk the resulting phoneme output from tokenization (as far as I can tell there isn't a limit on producing phonemes for arbitrary text lengths but i might be wrong). Rather than parse for sentences, where the resulting phoneme length will be unknown, parse the final phoneme output. This would require more work and a better understanding of the vocab but would help guarantee that chunks are aligned as close to 510 tokens as possible while preserving fluid speech. \n\nThese are just my back of the napkin two cents so definitely interested if others have additional ideas. ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
