    "updated_at": "2025-05-18T14:10:47Z",
    "body": "Thanks for the feedback so far. After more testing, I think the problem is specific to kokoro-js. I chunked a document with sentence boundaries and chunks of around 1000 characters. On the same machine, the python version of kokoro streams perfectly. The ONNX version (tested on multiple browsers) though, produces around 20%-30% broken audio. The issue seems to come from a memory problem, the browser console shows this:\n\n```\nvkAllocateMemory failed with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY\n - While calling [Device].CreateBuffer([BufferDescriptor]).\n    at CheckVkOOMThenSuccessImpl (..<URL>)\nUnderstand this warning\nkokoro-js.js?v=fc9b783d:9383 An uncaught WebGPU validation error was raised: [Invalid Buffer (unlabeled)] is invalid.\n - While validating entries[2] as a Buffer.\nExpected entry layout: {type: BufferBindingType::Storage, minBindingSize: 16, hasDynamicOffset: 0}\n - While validating [BindGroupDescriptor \"\"Mul\"\"] against [BindGroupLayout (unlabeled)]\n - While calling [Device].CreateBindGroup([BindGroupDescriptor \"\"Mul\"\"]).\n```\n\nThis error repeats multiple times. Then the next chunk is read correctly, until it happens again, seemingly random. If kokoro would throw an error when this happens, I could at least try to re-chunk with lower values, but seems like its a low level error and it does not get propagated.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
